course: dictionary
lesson: 6028
title: Working out a long dinosaur name
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Long, tricky words can look scary, but the words around them give you clues. «The first dinosaur to be scientifically named was Megalosaurus, in England in 1824.» The word dinosaur sits right next to it, so Megalosaurus must be a kind of dinosaur.
explain: The sentence also tells you when and where. It was named in England in 1824, so it was a real dinosaur from long ago. If you swap in your meaning, «The first large meat-eating dinosaur was a large meat-eating dinosaur, in England in 1824», and it still makes sense, so your guess is probably right.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Megalosaurus was a large meat-eating dinosaur.
fact: It lived in what is now England about 166 million years ago.
fact: It was the first dinosaur ever given a scientific name, in 1824.
fact: A scientific name is an official name that scientists use for a living thing or fossil.
exercise: «The first dinosaur to be scientifically named was Megalosaurus, in England in 1824.» What kind of thing is Megalosaurus? | A year | *A dinosaur | A scientist | A country
exercise: Megalosaurus was a large meat-eating dinosaur. What did it eat? | *Meat | Grass | Nuts | Leaves
exercise: «It was the first dinosaur ever given a scientific name, in 1824.» What does scientific name mean here? | *An official name used by scientists | A name from a famous film | A name painted on a fossil for fun | A nickname given by children
test: Megalosaurus lived in what is now England about 166 million years ago. Where did it live? | Canada | Egypt | *England | Australia
test: «It was the first dinosaur ever given a scientific name, in 1824.» In what year was Megalosaurus first given its scientific name? | 1842 | 1624 | 1866 | *1824
exam: «Megalosaurus was the first dinosaur ever given a scientific name.» Which statement is true? | It was a small bird | *It was a large meat-eating dinosaur | It was a fish | It was a plant
